# Add and use builtin kbdus.dll
# https://gitlab.winehq.org/wine/wine/-/merge_requests/12408 state=opened
# This simplifies a bit the mismatch between KBDTABLES in wow64 vs just native 32 bit, by just always assuming 64 bit sized pointers, similar to how currently all supported versions of windows ship their layout DLLs (we were already skipping some of the altgr test for this exact problematic configuration of 32 bit without wow64, so this makes sense I think).

To achieve this `__WINE_KBD_POINTER` acts sort of like `__ptr64` here conceptually. Also some refactoring with loading the KBDTABLES is happening in preparation for handling non-US layouts down the line as well (ideally through their respective registry entries).

Also compared to native DLLs there was a off-by one error that the first commit here fixes.

Some of this is maybe moving a bit into a different direction than https://gitlab.winehq.org/wine/wine/-/merge_requests/11909 (although some sort of caching should also need to be implemented; MSDN states changes to keyboard DLLs only take effect after a reboot), however I think it is long term better to have user drivers just selecting the correct HKL and handle everything else on the win32u side of things.

This also has the positive side-effect that win32 utilities that can inspect (and in some cases even modify) the keyboard layout are now working correctly too.
